<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Ivory Coast</h1><p class="st-meta">1893 CE – 1958 CE · capital Bingerville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French colony on the Gulf of Guinea that became the leading coffee and cocoa producer of French West Africa and independent Ivory Coast in 1960.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> signed treaties at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-bassam">Grand-Bassam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assinie">Assinie</a> in 1843, and merchants such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-verdier">Arthur Verdier</a> kept a French presence. In 1887-1889 the explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-gustave-binger">Louis-Gustave Binger</a> secured treaties in the interior, and in 1893 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> became a colony with Binger as governor. The capital moved from Grand-Bassam to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bingerville">Bingerville</a> in 1900 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abidjan">Abidjan</a> in 1934.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Conquest of the forest peoples was slow and violent, culminating in Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-angoulvant">Gabriel Angoulvant's</a> campaigns of 1908-1915. The colony, part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>, exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa, grown on European plantations and increasingly by African farmers using migrant labour from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-upper-volta">French Upper Volta</a>. Forced labour favoured European planters until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-agricultural-union">African Agricultural Union</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Felix Houphouet-Boigny</a>, founded in 1944, campaigned against it; it was abolished in 1946.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Houphouet-Boigny</a>, who also served as a minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, led the territory to autonomy within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> in 1958 and to independence on 7 August 1960. He remained president until his death in 1993, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abidjan">Abidjan</a> grew into a major West African metropolis.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1893 CE – 1958 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.37%;width:1.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1893 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bingerville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis-Gustave Binger, Gabriel Angoulvant, Felix Houphouet-Boigny</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Baoule, Dyula</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ivory Coast colony, Cote d'Ivoire (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1926&amp;focus=french-ivory-coast" aria-label="French Ivory Coast on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4572 1978 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Ivory Coast lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4892 2234L5054 728M4892 2234L4523 1888"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M4523 1888h0"/><pat